// An `override` is the same promise a conformance makes, one axis over: this callable stands in for that
// one. It was kept no better — all three positions compiled. A derived method is reached through the
// BASE's slot, so a changed signature means the caller's argument list and the callee's are two
// different lists. The damage is not theoretical: see override_arity_mismatch.
//
// The realistic way in is a base whose signature CHANGES — `int32 area()` becomes `isize area()` — while
// a subclass in another file, or in a consumer's package, still says `override fn int32 area()`.
type virtual(maxDepth: 1) resource Animal {
    public ctor make() { }
    protected virtual fn int32 own() { return 1; }
    public fn int32 total() { return this.own(); }
}
type final resource Dog extends Animal {
    public ctor make() { this.base = Base.make(); }
    protected override fn int64 own() { return 2; }      // base declares int32
}
fn int32 main() { Dog d = Dog.make(); return d.total() - 2; }
